    "result_text": "All 35 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a rule that rejects creating an owner whose lower-cased email is already used by any other owner, responding **409 Conflict**, following the existing `EnsureUniqueTelephone` pattern exactly.\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureUniqueEmail.java` \u2014 the pipeline step. Email is optional, so null/blank passes through; otherwise it lower-cases the request email and compares it against every existing owner's lower-cased email (`findAll()`), throwing `DuplicateEmailException` on a match.\n- `DuplicateEmailException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n- `DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `409` with body `{\"errors\":[\"email\"]}`, mirroring the telephone handler.\n- `escalation/\u2026DuplicateEmailException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wiring change:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `uniqueEmail` step between `email` (which normalizes/lower-cases the email) and `build`, under `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbors. Running after normalization means the comparison operates on the already-normalized value.\n\nThe uniqueness check runs against `findAll()` (deletes are hard deletes here, matching the telephone/household checks), and it lower-cases both sides so it's robust even against any non-normalized seed data.",
